it's no secret that over the past decade  warren buffett hasn't been at the top of  his game in fact between 2010 and 2020  he basically just matched the market  this isn't terrible performance given  that most hedge funds don't even match  the market but for a legendary investor  with an average annual return of 20.5  percent it's rather disappointing this  has caused many to wonder if buffett has  lost his touch in the new digital world  despite this lackluster performance over  the past decade  buffett has actually been crushing it in  2022 in fact berkshire hathaway is  slightly positive on the year while the  overall market has been getting  slaughtered currently berkshire hathaway  is up three percent on the year while  the s p 500 is down 16  this recent move means that berkshire  hathaway is officially outperforming the  market not just on a yearly basis but  since the bottom of the 2008 financial  recession which just when you quote  unquote lost its edge so here's the top  5 reasons why buffett has been  struggling to beat the market and how  he's overcoming these challenges  likely one of the biggest reasons  buffett has had trouble beating the s p  500 is due to his focus on value stocks  in recent history value stocks simply  haven't kept up with the growth stocks  in terms of returns between 2010 and  2020 growth stocks almost outperformed  value stocks by a full 100 percent  but while growth stocks tend to  outperform during bull markets they also  get crushed during bear markets and  given that the nasdaq is currently  experiencing a bear market it's not  surprising that many of these high  growth names have gotten destroyed since  the start of the year the growth stocks  in the s p 500 have underperformed  berkshire hathaway by about 25  conversely many value stocks have  performed extremely well such as energy  stocks since the start of the year the  value stocks in the s p 500 have  outperformed the overall index by nearly  10 percent so in the current market  environment buffett's value-centric  investing philosophy is holding up  pretty well aside from benefiting from a  risk-off market buffett has also moved  towards embracing a few high growth tech  companies himself if we take a look at  his holdings we'll see that apple is by  far his biggest position he currently  has 133 billion dollars in apple which  accounts for nearly 40 percent of his  entire portfolio another mega cap  company that buffett has recently  embraced is amazon his take in amazon  isn't nearly as large as his take in  apple but it is still a billion dollar  position buffett has even ventured into  some more early stage tech companies  such as snowflake which isn't even  profitable yet currently these stocks  are actually holding back buffett given  that they're all down by substantial  amounts apple is down 20 amazon is down  34 and snowflake is down over 50  so if buffet is able to post a positive  return with all these tech companies  being down so much imagine what type of  returns he'll be posting when these  companies recover  in the end buffett's willingness to  finally embrace tech while also  maintaining his value positions has  enabled him to enjoy the best of both  worlds  another major issue that buffett has  faced over the past decade is simply  putting his money to work buffett has  been holding significant amounts of cash  which of course produces little to no  returns his cash position has gone  slightly better over the past two years  as it has dropped from 128 billion  dollars to 106 billion dollars today but  there's still a long way to go but wait  a minute why is buffett even having  trouble deploying his cash in the first  place well at the core buffett is still  a value investor and though he has  embraced some growth plays he's not  willing to buy when prices are quote sky  high now you might disagree with the  statement given the recent sell-off but  on a historical basis the market is  still trading for a rich valuation for  example the median p e ratio of s p 500  companies over the past 140 years is  14.89 today the p e ratio of the s p 500  stands at 20.34 which means that the  average company is still overvalued by  33 percent  similarly the buffett indicator which  compares the us stock market with the  u.s gdp suggests that stocks are  currently overvalued by 75  now i'm sure there's gonna be comments  down below that argued that these models  are outdated due to low interest rates  excessive money printing and the rise of  international markets and while you may  be right we have to acknowledge that  buffett is more of an old-school  investor  so i think it's safe to assume that he  gives at least some weight to the  indicator that was literally named after  him alright so the overall market may  seem overvalued from buffett's  perspective but regardless of the market  environment there's always deals on the  market right well there are always deals  on the market but the problem is that  these are generally underrated small cap  companies that won't really move the  needle for buffett for example let's say  he finds an extraordinary small cap  company that's worth one billion dollars  even if buffett bought this entire  company and the company include 10x  within the next 10 years he would only  make 9 billion dollars now that's an  insane amount of money but it would only  grow buffett's overall portfolio by a  mere 2.6 percent considering this  investing in small cap companies is  simply not a feasible option for buffett  he has to stick to companies that are  worth tens of billions if not hundreds  of billions and amongst these companies  it's been extremely difficult to spot a  deal one of the main ways that buffett  has been compounding this issue is by  constantly growing his existing  positions last quarter for example he  bought 600 million dollars worth of  apple stock but this can only take him  so far and sooner or later he's going to  have to find new companies to invest in  as buffett's portfolio continues to grow  and he concentrates on large cap  companies one of the biggest hurdles he  has to face is unintentionally becoming  the market himself i mean if you just  keep adding large cup positions you  basically just become the snp 500  yourself buffett currently has 48  positions in basically every industry  you can think of from tech and retail to  food and banking while this may not  sound like a big deal it is given that  buffet is currently breaking his own  advice  one of buffett's most famous sayings is  that diversification is protection  against ignorance it makes little sense  if you know what you're doing  now i don't think buffett is  diversifying to protect against  ignorance i think he's diversifying in  an attempt to deploy as much capital as  possible but this naturally comes along  with the unintended consequence of  limiting upside potential aside from  breaking his own diversification role  buffet is also actively breaking his 20  slot rule the rule suggests that an  individual's financial success could be  improved if they could only make 20  investments throughout their entire life  the idea is that if an individual's  investment choices are heavily  constrained they would be highly  selective with each investment they make  and hopefully this electiveness would  improve their chances of making good  investments given that buffett is  currently holding on to 48 different  positions he's clearly not abiding by  this role but this isn't to say that  buffett isn't trying his best to limit  diversification  even though buffett has dozens of  positions his portfolio is still  extremely top heavy his top 10 positions  for example account for 85 percent of  his portfolio and his top 20 positions  account for 96 of his portfolio in other  words all of buffett's investments past  the 20 slot roll only account for four  percent of his portfolio despite all of  his efforts though it's only natural  that the performance of berkshire  hathaway gets closer and closer to that  of the market as they continue to grow  and their investments mature  on top of all these external factors  weighing down on berkshire's performance  it should be noted that buffett has made  a few major mistakes himself for example  in the early 2010s buffett wanted to get  in on all the tech growth so he decided  to buy a legacy player which was ibm in  2011 he announced that he purchased 10.7  billion worth of ibm at 170 dollars per  share while ibm did reach about 200 per  share in 2013 he has since been on a  decline and buffett eventually ended up  selling his shares for a loss while this  was no doubt a subpar investment buffett  also made one of the worst investments  of his career within the past decade  which is of course craft heinz while  buffett maintains that both kraft and  heinz are great companies fundamentally  he has admitted that he overpaid for the  craft portion of the business aside from  paying a premium for the company buffett  went on to oversee a merger of the two  companies which hasn't been all that  successful i have a full video on the  downfall of craft heinz if you're  interested but in essence the company  couldn't keep up with the prices of  generic fans or the healthiness of  up-and-coming brands so they've been  stuck in this weird middle ground that  doesn't appeal to either demographic on  top of this craft heinz has been  involved in an accounting scandal  they've had to write off massive losses  and the ceo left during the peak of  their troubles as you would guess all of  these issues have resulted in craft  heinz stock getting obliterated they're  currently down over 50 percent from  their peak in 2017. despite this  horrendous performance buffett has  continued to vouch for kraft heinz but  it should also be noted that he has been  transparent that he would have sold the  stock a long time ago if he was able to  easily liquidate his massive position  fortunately though it does look like  kraft heinz has finally bottomed out and  it's starting to form an upward trend so  hopefully craft heinz won't weigh down  on buffett's portfolio moving forward  while everything we've covered so far in  the video are valid points something  that we shouldn't overlook is buffett's  age buffett is currently 91 years old  this isn't to say that buffett is too  old for this or anything like that but  it does bring up the question how much  does buffett even care about beating the  market anymore when you're over 90 years  old i suspect that you would care a lot  more about living out the last of your  days to the best of your ability as  opposed to beating some vain metric  buffett clearly still enjoys investing  and talking about stocks that's why he  continues to lead berkshire and  regularly participates in press events  but with that being said i think buffett  has several more priorities that are  higher than beating the market like  philanthropy warren buffett is the  biggest philanthropist in the entire  world having donated a massive 42.8  billion dollars and a given that buffett  is looking to donate all of his wealth  after he dies i suspect that his number  one priority is to ensure that all of  his wealth ends up in the right hands  this aligns with what he's been  recommending as well after all buffett  doesn't even recommend that you invest  in berkshire he recommends that you  invest in the s p 500 considering this  it's pretty clear that buffett isn't  exactly trying to appeal to investors  meaning that maximizing returns is not  his top priority at this point it seems  like he's just looking to see how far he  can take it while having some fun and it  just so happens that he's beating the  market once again do you guys think that  this is just some random fluke or do you  think buffett will continue to leave the  market comment that down below also drop  a like if you're a fan of buffett's  insane philanthropy 